                {
                  "code": "SIMPLIFY_AE_102",
                  "title": "Simplifying Algebraic Expressions",
                  "image": "simplify_ae.jpg",
                  "duration": 800,
                  "content": "Simplifying algebraic expressions involves combining like terms and applying the rules of arithmetic. Like terms are terms that have the same variable raised to the same power. For example, in the expression (4x + 3x - 2x), the like terms are (4x), (3x), and (-2x), which can be combined to give (5x). To simplify expressions with parentheses, use the distributive property: (a(b + c) = ab + ac). For example, (3(x + 4) = 3x + 12). Simplifying expressions makes them easier to work with and is a key step in solving equations."
                },
                {
