                  "image": "solve_ae.jpg",
                  "duration": 800,
                  "content": "Solving algebraic equations involves finding the value of the variable that satisfies the equation. For linear equations (equations of the form (ax + b = c)), the goal is to isolate the variable on one side of the equation. For example, to solve (3x + 5 = 20), subtract 5 from both sides to get (3x = 15), then divide both sides by 3 to get (x = 5). For quadratic equations (equations of the form (ax^2 + bx + c = 0)), methods such as factoring, completing the square, or using the quadratic formula can be used. Solving equations is essential for analyzing relationships and making predictions in mathematics and real-world applications."
