      {
        "title": "Simultaneous Equations",
        "code": "SE_101",
        "image": "simultaneous_equations.jpg",
        "topics": [
          {
            "title": "Understanding Simultaneous Equations",
            "code": "SE_101",
            "image": "understanding_se.jpg",
            "materials": {
              "notes": [
                {
                  "code": "INTRO_SE_101",
                  "title": "Introduction to Simultaneous Equations",
                  "image": "intro_se.jpg",
                  "duration": 800,
                  "content": "Simultaneous equations are a set of equations with multiple variables that are solved together to find a common solution. For example, the system of equations 2x + y = 10 and x - y = 2 are simultaneous equations. The solution to these equations is the pair of values for x and y that satisfy both equations simultaneously. Simultaneous equations are used to model real-world problems where multiple conditions must be satisfied at the same time, such as in economics, physics, and engineering."
                },
                {
                  "code": "METHODS_SE_102",
                  "title": "Methods for Solving Simultaneous Equations",
                  "image": "methods_se.jpg",
                  "duration": 800,
                  "content": "There are several methods for solving simultaneous equations, including substitution, elimination, and graphical methods. Substitution Method: Solve one equation for one variable and substitute this expression into the other equation. For example, solve x - y = 2 for x to get x = y + 2, then substitute into 2x + y = 10 to solve for y. Elimination Method: Add or subtract the equations to eliminate one variable. For example, add 2x + y = 10 and x - y = 2 to eliminate y and solve for x. Graphical Method: Plot both equations on a graph and find the point of intersection. Each method has its advantages depending on the complexity of the equations."
                },
                {
                  "code": "APPL_SE_103",
