        "title": "Inequalities",
        "code": "INEQ_101",
        "image": "inequalities.jpg",
        "topics": [
          {
            "title": "Understanding Inequalities",
            "code": "INEQ_101",
            "image": "understanding_ineq.jpg",
            "materials": {
              "notes": [
                {
                  "code": "INTRO_INEQ_101",
                  "title": "Introduction to Inequalities",
                  "image": "intro_ineq.jpg",
                  "duration": 800,
                  "content": "Inequalities are mathematical expressions that compare two values or expressions using symbols such as < (less than), > (greater than), ≤ (less than or equal to), and ≥ (greater than or equal to). They are used to represent relationships where one quantity is not equal to another. Inequalities are widely used in algebra, optimization problems, and real-world applications such as economics and engineering."
                },
                {
                  "code": "SOLVE_INEQ_102",
                  "title": "Solving Linear Inequalities",
                  "image": "solve_ineq.jpg",
                  "duration": 800,
                  "content": "Solving linear inequalities involves finding the range of values that satisfy the inequality. For example, to solve 2x + 3 > 7, subtract 3 from both sides to get 2x > 4, then divide by 2 to get x > 2. The solution is all real numbers greater than 2. When multiplying or dividing both sides of an inequality by a negative number, the inequality sign must be reversed."
                },
                {
                  "code": "GRAPH_INEQ_103",
                  "title": "Graphing Inequalities",
                  "image": "graph_ineq.jpg",
                  "duration": 800,
                  "content": "Graphing inequalities on a number line or coordinate plane helps visualize the solution set. For example, the inequality x > 2 is represented by an open circle at 2 and a shaded line extending to the right. For two-variable inequalities like y < 2x + 1, the graph is a shaded region below the line y = 2x + 1."
                }
              ],
              "videos": [],
              "audios": [],
              "tests": [
