                {
                  "code": "ANGLES_GEO_102",
                  "title": "Angles and Triangles",
                  "image": "angles_triangles.jpg",
                  "duration": 800,
                  "content": "Angles are formed by two rays sharing a common endpoint. Triangles are three-sided polygons with three angles. The sum of the interior angles of a triangle is always 180 degrees. Triangles can be classified by their sides (equilateral, isosceles, scalene) or by their angles (acute, right, obtuse)."
                },
                {
                  "code": "CIRCLES_GEO_103",
                  "title": "Circles and Their Properties",
                  "image": "circles.jpg",
                  "duration": 800,
                  "content": "A circle is a set of all points in a plane that are equidistant from a fixed point called the center. Key properties of circles include radius, diameter, circumference, and area. The circumference of a circle is given by C = 2πr, and the area is A = πr², where r is the radius."
                }
