                {
                  "code": "VECT_BASIC_101",
                  "title": "Basic Concepts of Vectors",
                  "image": "basic_vectors.jpg",
                  "duration": 900,
                  "content": "A vector is a quantity having both magnitude and direction. It is represented by an arrow pointing from one point to another. Vectors are used in physics and engineering to represent forces, velocities, and other directional quantities."
                },
                {
                  "code": "VECT_OPER_102",
                  "title": "Vector Operations",
                  "image": "vector_operations.jpg",
                  "duration": 900,
                  "content": "Vector operations include addition, subtraction, and scalar multiplication. The sum of two vectors is found using the triangle or parallelogram rule. Scalar multiplication scales the vector's magnitude without changing its direction."
                },
                {
                  "code": "VECT_DOT_CROSS_103",
                  "title": "Dot Product and Cross Product",
                  "image": "dot_cross_products.jpg",
                  "duration": 900,
                  "content": "The dot product of two vectors measures how much one vector extends in the direction of another. The cross product gives a vector perpendicular to both input vectors, useful in calculating areas of parallelograms and torque."
                }
              ],
