        "code": "VOICE_101",
        "image": "active_passive_voice.jpg",
        "topics": [
          {
            "title": "Understanding Active and Passive Voice",
            "code": "VOICE_101",
            "image": "understanding_voice.jpg",
            "materials": {
              "notes": [
                {
                  "code": "ACTIVE_VOICE_101",
                  "title": "Active Voice",
                  "image": "active_voice.jpg",
                  "duration": 1000,
                  "content": "Active voice is a grammatical structure where the subject performs the action stated by the verb. This form of sentence construction is direct, clear, and often more impactful. For example: 'The teacher explained the lesson.' The subject (the teacher) performs the action (explained). Active voice is commonly used in most forms of writing because it adds clarity and energy to sentences."
                },
                {
                  "code": "PASSIVE_VOICE_102",
                  "title": "Passive Voice",
                  "image": "passive_voice.jpg",
                  "duration": 1000,
                  "content": "Passive voice occurs when the subject receives the action expressed by the verb. It often includes a form of 'to be' with the past participle of the main verb. For example: 'The lesson was explained by the teacher.' The focus shifts from the doer (the teacher) to the receiver (the lesson). Passive voice is useful when the performer of the action is unknown or unimportant."
                },
                {
                  "code": "RULES_VOICE_103",
                  "title": "Rules for Converting Active to Passive Voice",
                  "image": "rules_voice.jpg",
                  "duration": 1200,
                  "content": "Rules for converting active to passive voice:  1. Identify the subject, verb, and object in the active voice. 2. Move the object to the subject position. 3. Use the appropriate form of the verb 'to be' and the past participle of the main verb. 4. Optionally include the doer of the action using 'by'. 5. Ensure the tense remains consistent, for example:   - Present simple: She writes a letter. -> A letter is written by her.   - Past simple: He painted the house. -> The house was painted by him."
                }
              ],
              "videos": [],
              "audios": [],
